Usage: issue sync [--all] [--no-clean]

Reconcile the local directory registry with the provider state (orchestrator-only). Candidates are the issue associations whose registered directory still exists; each candidate's remote state is read, and one the provider already closed converges exactly the way `issue close` converges it locally: its directories run the same close guards (clean, never the main checkout, every other associated task confirmed closed, fresh host occupancy), and a directory a guard keeps stays on disk with the guard's reason in the report. Branches are never deleted and registry rows are never discarded while a directory still exists.

Default scope is the repository of the current working directory. --all scans every repository identity recorded in the directory registry through its main checkout; a checkout that is missing or resolves to a different identity is reported under skipped_repos instead of being guessed. --no-clean is the report mode: the same candidates and the same local guard verdicts with per-directory would_clean/would_keep actions, and no write at all. Each candidate issue is read through its own durable task, which pins its provider and instance; a legacy number without a durable task keeps the configured-provider resolution. Clean mode proves the exact issue closed and re-reads fresh scope evidence before removal, while --no-clean never removes a directory.

A directory associated with an OpenCode session (`ses_…`) is removed only once the host confirms no session is still hosted there, and the pass never moves a session: a close that could not return one leaves its directory behind, and this pass removes it once the host reports the session elsewhere. An incomplete or unreachable host answer, a missing associated idle session still named by the current listing, or a typed non-absence failure keeps the directory with its reason. In report mode an OpenCode-associated directory is always would_keep and the host is not contacted at all.

stdout is one JSON envelope {mode, all, checked, not_closed, not_found, cleaned, kept, skipped_repos?, issues:[...]}. A remote read failure is a structured error with a non-zero exit that deletes nothing, while a remote 404 counts into not_found and the pass continues. `worktree acquire` and `worktree list` run the same pass for their repository before doing their own work and append its warnings to stderr; --no-sync skips that pass. `worktree prune` never runs a sync pass; its --no-sync spelling is a compatibility no-op.

`phasegent worktree prune --remove` is the explicit removal path described by `phasegent --help worktree prune`.

Close an issue
